  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are nonzero numbers such that \\(x=1+\\dfrac1y\\) and \\(y=1+\\dfrac1x,\\) then \\(y\\) equals:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x-1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(1-x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(1+x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(-x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(x\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1968-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The equations give \\(xy=y+1\\) and \\(xy=x+1.\\) Therefore \\(x+1=y+1,\\) so \\(y=x.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Multiply each equation by its denominator",
        "Both equations produce an expression for \\(xy\\)"
      ],
